          "context": "Repetition-loop degeneration: gpt2-small under greedy decoding collapses into verbatim phrase loops (Holtzman et al. 2019 'neural text degeneration'; Xu et al. 2022 show P(repeat) self-reinforces with the number of prior in-context occurrences — the induction signature). Break the loop by cutting the model's SELF-copy cells for the looping phrase — (query = current loop-phrase token × key = token-after-previous-occurrence) — instead of penalizing the repeated tokens globally.",
          "mechanism": "A loop is the induction conjunction applied to the model's own output. Both endpoints are ordinary tokens with high legitimate reuse (names, terms, anaphora legitimately repeat); the query content is the specific phrase token (distinctive content, not a role), and the conjunction 'this token, in a context where its previous occurrence is being continued' is the loop itself. Direct consumption: the looped token IS the attended token's OV write.",
          "why_linear_steer_fails": "The deployed fixes are precisely marginal-endpoint penalties — frequency/repetition penalty or token-direction suppression — which damage every legitimate reuse of those tokens and degrade fluency globally (the documented cost of repetition penalties). A content-gated steer at the phrase tokens wrecks the whole residual that all consumers read at those positions. FRA cuts only the self-copy edge, leaving legitimate (non-loop) reuse of the same tokens intact.",
          "model_benchmark": "gpt2-small + Holtzman 2019 / Xu et al. 2022 repetition metrics (rep-n, loop rate, distinct-n) on WebText/OpenWebText prompts under greedy decoding; res-jb SAEs all layers. LBNR check: does cutting induction-edge cells at the loop tokens break the loop (vs MLP/logit self-reinforcement)?",
